  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Texas received $78.1B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 161,134 awards with an average award size of $485K. Obligations fell 9.5% year over year. Fiscal-year totals show variation: FY21 was $55.1B, FY22 was $67.6B, FY23 was $81.9B, FY24 was $59.3B, and FY25 was $86.3B. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in Texas with $31.9B in obligations, followed by the Department of Homeland Security with $25B and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $16.1B. McKesson Corporation and Lockheed Martin Corporation are the top vendors with $11B and $10.6B respectively, and a second Lockheed Martin listing at $7.3B also appears among the top suppliers. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry by obligations at $17.6B. This snapshot covers federal contract obligations through the current fiscal year to date and represents partial-year totals.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies are buying the most in Texas?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $31.9B in obligations. The Department of Homeland Security follows with $25B, and the Department of Veterans Affairs has $16.1B. Other notable buyers include the Department of Energy at $1.7B and NASA at $1.3B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest contractors and which industries dominate?",
        "body": "MCKESSON CORPORATION leads vendors with $11B, followed by LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION at $10.6B and a second LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION entry at $7.3B. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry with $17.6B, and Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing accounts for $11B. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing and Aircraft Manufacturing are also major industries at $10.1B and $9.8B respectively."
      }
    ]
  },
